  "question": {
    "text": "Let a function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8469;</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8469;</mi></math>&nbsp;be defined by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"\" open=\"[\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>n</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math><br />then, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi></math>&nbsp;is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "One-one and onto",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "One-one but not onto",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Onto but not one-one",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Neither one-one nor onto",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>For one value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> we will get only one corresponding value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced></math>, so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced></math> is one-one</p><p>Now, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#8943;</mo><mo>;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#8943;</mo><mo>;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#8943;</mo><mo>;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>So range of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math></p><p>Hence&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mfenced><mi>n</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is onto</p>"
  }
}